Expected elements: present

I checked the three additions independently. The `Age` row is there and states `No timestamp published` rather than deriving one; the `Type` row reads `public`; the full hash sits in a bordered, raised box with `click to select` beside it. The provenance note now sets flush-left as its own grid-spanning row at a ~75-character measure — the flush-right 28-character column four reviewers named last round is gone, and this is a competent paragraph now. Fee and cost are base ten. The decoded-input note states the actual state (unknown selector, no call data, therefore nothing to decode), and the absence of a supply-an-ABI control follows from it rather than contradicting it. One provenance marker, the `Data` row, in the correct form. All eight sections carry a statement, no empty panel, no Debug button, no disabled stand-in.

The single weakest element is the `Pending` badge in the `FINALITY` row of the Overview grid. It is the fact that decides whether anything above it can still change, and it is drawn as the least prominent token on the page: a neutral grey pill in the sixth row, cut from the same die as `Yes` in `CANONICAL` directly above it and `No timestamp published` directly above that, so one shape carries a settlement status, a boolean and an absence statement in three consecutive rows. Meanwhile the only coloured status token on the page — the green `Succeeded` — sits over 800 px higher in the hero, unqualified. A reader who scans the hero and stops, which is what a hero is for, leaves believing this transaction is settled. The page holds both facts and is truthful in both; it has simply given all of the emphasis to the half that cannot change and none to the half that can.

That is why it is the weakest: not that it is ugly, but that the page's own hierarchy argues against its own data.

